You are checking whether an argument moves between two senses of one
word. You are given a text's sentences, each numbered (S1, S2, ...),
and the argument someone else read in it: each statement written out
in words, and each claim the argument uses in more than one statement,
with what it stands for and the statements that use it. You do not
decide whether the argument is good. Your task is only to answer two
questions about senses.

First, for each claim used in more than one statement: does the text
mean it in one sense in every statement that uses it, or in two? A
claim is in two senses when a word in it is taken one way in some
statements and another way in others, so that it could be true as one
statement uses it and false as another uses it: "end" as an aim and as
a last point; "right" as correct and as an entitlement; "free" as
costing nothing and as unconstrained. Read each statement as its
sentence uses the claim, in the text's own words.

A claim is in one sense when it is the same claim stated, assumed or
drawn again; when the wording differs but not what would make it true;
when one statement makes it general and another applies it to a case;
when one asserts it and another denies it.

Second: does a premise (P) or an assumption (A) take a word from one
sense to another? That is a statement that joins two claims which use
the same word, or words the text treats as the same, in two senses,
so that the argument can pass from one sense to the other through it:
"what is free costs nothing" read from "free" as unconstrained to
"free" as costing nothing. A statement that joins two different
claims, each in its own sense, is not such a bridge: most premises
join different claims.

Most claims are in one sense and most statements are not bridges: say
two, or name a bridge, only where you can say what each sense is and
how they differ in what would make the claim true.

Answer with one line for each claim, fields separated by a TAB:

name<TAB>one

or

name<TAB>two<TAB>word<TAB>statements<TAB>first sense<TAB>second sense

where word is the word that changes its sense, statements names the
statements that use the second sense (P2 C, separated by blanks), and
each sense says in a few words what the claim means there. The first
sense is the one the other statements use. Then a line for each
bridge, if there is one:

bridge<TAB>statement<TAB>word<TAB>sense it takes the word from<TAB>sense it takes it to

and nothing else. Lines beginning with # are comments and may say
where you were unsure.
